 Figure~\ref{fig:circulation} plots $j_t$ for the three assets and Table~\ref{tab:circulation} summarizes it. The scalar is negative at every one of the 2,766 evaluation origins for every asset, with means of $-1.40$, $-0.77$ and $-1.32$ percentage points of daily flow. In the period-average matrices the three pairwise differences have nearly equal magnitude: for SPY, $P(\text{Up}\mid\text{Down})-P(\text{Down}\mid\text{Up})=+4.2$ and the two other pairs are $-4.2$ percentage points; the corresponding magnitudes are 2.3 for QQQ and 4.0 for IWM. The reversible fit imposes $\pi_iP_{ij}=\pi_jP_{ji}$. With the estimated stationary distribution that close to uniform, its fitted entries are within $3\times10^{-4}$ of pairwise averages, and it shifts individual off-diagonal entries by 1.2 to 2.1 percentage points. That near-equality of entries is a property of this estimate, not of the estimator: when the stationary distribution is not uniform, detailed balance does not imply $P_{ij}=P_{ji}$.
 
-The series is highly persistent, with first-order autocorrelation between 0.990 and 0.997. The agreement in sign across origins is therefore not 2,766 independent confirmations; it describes one persistent feature of this sample, and the annual means move by at most a few tenths of a point. Removing that feature costs 0.0007 to 0.0016 nats of log loss, an interval that contains zero (Table~\ref{tab:intervals}). The fitted chain carries the asymmetry at every origin and the restriction removes it by construction, but whether the population chain does so cannot be settled here: an expanding-window path reuses almost all of its history between adjacent origins, so persistence is expected even if the underlying circulation is zero. No test of $j=0$ in the population is performed. Section~\ref{sec:simulation} quantifies the cost of the restriction under generating processes whose circulation is known; that explains a mechanism under stated assumptions and does not identify it in these data.
